  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/6115a87572fb93b9e3dcf69c75cd4a5efaae2dbbea90b28cb57cdd05b8db11b7.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=2.$ For any open interval containing $x=2$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/26acee415fa4a1038077d11f88b7c61c8b45f4aa857cc578af192e4085f7c005.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
